ll open it up and you can pepper them with your questions.

00:02:30.889 --> 00:02:34.949
Alright. So, just let me say a little bit about the framing

00:02:34.969 --> 00:02:38.349
of the topic and the question of building a distinct company

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.749
culture. So, the underlying premise is a distinct culture is

00:02:41.769 --> 00:02:45.949
important to the success of a new venture and that

00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:48.439
successful entrepreneurs are very deliberate about creating

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:52.609
and building a culture that supports their success. There

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:55.449
are strategists who will tell you that in order for a

00:02:55.469 --> 00:02:58.429
culture to be a source of competitive advantage it needs to

00:02:58.449 --> 00:03:00.339
have three different things.

00:03:00.359 --> 00:03:03.779
It's got to be valuable to the organization - a source of

00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:07.299
cost saving differentiation and revenue.

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:10.029
It's got to be rare - the unique piece.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:14.169
I have got to be difficult to imitate or copy. Otherwise

00:03:14.189 --> 00:03:16.509
anybody could do it.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:20.459
So, what I'd like to do is just give the panelists a chance

00:03:20.479 --> 00:03:23.479
to start out and just say a little bit about yourself, and

00:03:23.479 --> 00:03:27.579
your company, and tell us how would you describe - and how

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:30.779
do people generally describe - the culture of your

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:34.759
organization? And how is that related to its competitive

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:35.949
strategy?

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:39.759
So, I could just go down from the right or is anybody

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.869
burning to? I think Scott kind of got nominated by Amy

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:46.489
originally to go first. So, we'll jump around.

00:03:46.509 --> 00:03:51.659
Thank you. Cuckolded. I haven't been back in a long time and

00:03:51.679 --> 00:03:55.789
get cuckolded. So, my background is primarily as a

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.499
technologist. So, I've been a co - founder in three public

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:01.549
companies, two software companies, and one

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:04.859
telecommunications company. And I'm now CEO of a fourth

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:09.439
which is venture funded stealth. And I can tell you that the

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:12.319
big epiphany I had - prior to coming to business school I'd

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:15.749
founded two companies and both companies grew quite large -

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:17.949
hundreds and hundreds of people, different locations.

00:04:17.969 --> 00:04:21.610
And one of the experiences I had before I came was I visited

00:04:21.629 --> 00:04:24.720
an outlying office that I had. And I remember sitting in a

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:28.360
meeting and about halfway through it all I could think was,

00:04:28.379 --> 00:04:31.689
"How did these people get in my company?" What I realized

00:04:31.709 --> 00:04:34.459
was that the core group that we had started with was

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:37.850
something that reflected the values of the founders in a big

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:42.009
way. And as the company grew very quickly we lacked the

00:04:42.029 --> 00:04:45.329
methodology and the structure so that the culture could

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:47.740
remain consistent as the company grew.

00:04:47.759 --> 00:04:51.170
And at that moment when we thought about creating a new

00:04:51.189 --> 00:04:53.599
company, one of the things that became very important was

00:04:53.599 --> 00:04:56.259
trying to create the culture that I wanted, something that

00:04:56.279 --> 00:04:58.709
was durable, and something that was scalable. So, we

00:04:58.729 --> 00:05:03.120
actually spent a lot of time in that process of, you know,

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:05.219
trying to come up with something that fit for all the

00:05:05.219 --> 00:05:06.219
founders.

00:05:06.219 --> 00:05:09.169
And in terms of creating value, it would take too long to

00:05:09.169 --> 00:05:12.099
share everything in first blush. But I can tell you the last

00:05:12.099 --> 00:05:13.099
company,

00:05:13.099 --> 00:05:16.160
[inaudible] which became a public company as well - we had

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:21.009
the lowest turnover in the industry by far in our segment.

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:24.509
Our average salary was at the 50th percentile, you know,

00:05:24.529 --> 00:05:29.050
compared to our peers. And you know, the level of

00:05:29.069 --> 00:05:30.669
satisfaction, the productivity we got out was really just

00:05:30.669 --> 00:05:31.669
great.

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:35.170
So you know, there are very tangible benefits to investing

00:05:35.189 --> 00:05:38.189
in culture - some of which you can create, some of which are

00:05:38.189 --> 00:05:41.280
basically by virtue of your actions and so forth. But the

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.969
net is you're going to get a culture either way. But we

00:05:43.969 --> 00:05:46.660
tried to be more premeditated about doing it at

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.680
[inaudible].

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.120
And if you were to sort of think of the sworn to

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:52.409
characteristics that are really like, "Okay. This is what's

00:05:52.409 --> 00:05:55.550
really different about our culture...what stands out?"

00:05:55.569 --> 00:06:00.240
Probably the single biggest thing is we became I guess

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:03.589
passionate about feedback while we were here at the GSB - so

00:06:03.609 --> 00:06:06.849
touchy, feely. I don't know how many of you are GSB alums

00:06:06.849 --> 00:06:10.009
that have taken that. And we all had been in some

00:06:10.029 --> 00:06:13.079
bureaucratic environments previously and saw feedback -

00:06:13.099 --> 00:06:17.360
institutionalizing it as a method of being able to increase

00:06:17.379 --> 00:06:21.079
the velocity of decision making, reduce bureaucracy, and

00:06:21.099 --> 00:06:23.459
ultimately resolve issues before they become big.

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:26.709
So, we spent a lot of time implementing a feedback structure

00:06:26.729 --> 00:06:30.250
throughout the company and it grew with the company. And

00:06:30.269 --> 00:06:32.829
quite frankly, we use it with our new company as well. I

00:06:32.829 --> 00:06:35.329
think that was probably the single most impactful tool we

00:06:35.329 --> 00:06:38.040
had at addressing issues when they came up immediately and

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.110
not letting them fester.

00:06:40.129 --> 00:06:42.449
Okay. Amy? Do you want to...?

00:06:42.469 --> 00:06:47.300
Sure. My name is Amy Pressman. I'm the Co - Founder and

00:06:47.319 --> 00:06:50.000
President of a company called Madelia. We are a technology

00:06:50.019 --> 00:06:53.980
company that provides technology that kind of wires really

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.259
large organizations to their voice of the customer. So, we

00:06:57.279 --> 00:06:59.930
collect feedback, we track social media and we tell the

00:06:59.949 --> 00:07:02.759
companies how they're doing.

00:07:02.779 --> 00:07:05.500
A couple of things about us - we're bootstrapped. So, we

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.189
have not taken a dime of money. We're a little bit smaller

00:07:08.189 --> 00:07:10.939
than probably some of the other people here at the table.

00:07:10.939 --> 00:07:15.360
But we grew to $25 million so far without taking any money.

00:07:15.379 --> 00:07:18.589
And I didn't think about culture at all when we were

00:07:18.609 --> 00:07:21.870
started. I think most startups - unless you have really,

00:07:21.889 --> 00:07:25.269
really crazy co - founders - probably have pretty good

00:07:25.289 --> 00:07:28.389
cultures because just by their nature you've got a bunch of

00:07:28.409 --> 00:07:31.649
people that, you know, are totally energized by an idea,

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:34.589
they're coming together. They're in a garage. They want to

00:07:34.589 --> 00:07:37.089
change the world, maybe they'll make a lot of money at the

00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:38.089
same time.

00:07:38.089 --> 00:07:40.589
It's kind of a motivating and empowering thing. And people

00:07:40.589 --> 00:07:43.589
tend to have fairly good cultures and then you hear a pretty

00:07:43.589 --> 00:07:46.000
typical story that, you know, they meet a lot of milestones

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.009
and with each milestone, you know, they'll get more money,

00:07:49.029 --> 00:07:52.000
more professionals will come in and somehow the culture isn'

00:07:52.019 --> 00:07:55.519
t quite the same as it was back when we were 10 people or

00:07:55.519 --> 00:07:57.019
back when we were 50 people.

00:07:57.019 --> 00:08:00.019
So, I think we were much the same. We didn't think much

00:08:00.019 --> 00:08:02.629
about our culture. We did have similar very low turnover,

00:08:02.649 --> 00:08:06.699
very high employee satisfaction scores. We've won awards for

00:08:06.719 --> 00:08:08.779
being one of the best workplaces.

00:08:08.799 --> 00:08:13.389
But I would say the two things that really, really made me

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:14.659
think about culture are this:

00:08:14.659 --> 00:08:23.959
Because we were bootstrapping we didn't have a full fledged

00:08:23.979 --> 00:08:27.759
team when we started. So, we ended up hiring a Head of Sales

00:08:27.779 --> 00:08:31.029
about five years into our existence and he brought with him

00:08:31.049 --> 00:08:35.689
a sales team. And it came all from another company. So, it's

00:08:35.709 --> 00:08:38.929
a little bit like a mini - merger. You take the culture of

00:08:38.949 --> 00:08:42.199
one company and then you sort of graft it on to our company.

00:08:42.199 --> 00:08:45.129
And we did the same thing with our marketing team. We hired

00:08:45.129 --> 00:08:47.220
a head of marketing who ended up bringing a bunch of people

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.470
from her company.

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:52.240
And it is when you start to see this juxtaposition of these

00:08:52.259 --> 00:08:54.720
various cultures that you start to realize, "Oh, this is

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.009
what our culture is and this is how it's different from

00:08:57.009 --> 00:08:58.009
other cultures.

00:08:58.009 --> 00:09:02.960
So, that was, you know, one thing that made me really aware

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:06.899
of it. And I think the other thing is that because of the

00:09:06.919 --> 00:09:11.360
product that we have - which is customer experience tracking

00:09:11.379 --> 00:09:14.960
- and because we were an unknown startup, we went out and

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.419
sold our product to some really well - known brands in the

00:09:17.419 --> 00:09:20.279
company. It turned out that one of those were companies that

00:09:20.299 --> 00:09:22.889
cared very much about the customer experience and were

00:09:22.909 --> 00:09:26.179
willing to take a chance on an unknown startup. And those

00:09:26.199 --> 00:09:28.830
tended to be most admired companies.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.850
So, we have this A+ list of customers. We have things like

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:34.459
Apple leading retail, we have Four Seasons and hotels. We've

00:09:34.459 --> 00:09:37.929
got companies that care deeply about the customer experience

00:09:37.949 --> 00:09:41.279
and we decided that we were going to go out and see what

00:09:41.299 --> 00:09:45.580
they did. And we not only looked at these companies at a

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.879
company level and at a brand level, we also - because we

00:09:48.899 --> 00:09:52.309
track their customer experience scores - went to the highest

00:09:52.329 --> 00:09:56.279
performing outlets, for example Gold's Gym - the highest

00:09:56.299 --> 00:09:59.189
performing Gold's Gym outlet, the highest performing Hilton

00:09:59.209 --> 00:10:00.209
Hotel.

00:10:00.209 --> 00:10:03.059
And we sat down and we talked to their General Manager's.

00:10:03.059 --> 00:10:06.179
And we started to find a really consistent thing and that is

00:10:06.199 --> 00:10:10.889
that they had extremely strong cultures that the management

00:10:10.909 --> 00:10:13.919
spent an incredible amount of time nurturing. And what I

00:10:13.939 --> 00:10:17.919
realized is all companies have cultures. Whether you decide

00:10:17.939 --> 00:10:22.279
to focus on it or not your company will have a culture. When

00:10:22.299 --> 00:10:27.250
you spend a lot of time on it and you align the way that you

00:10:27.269 --> 00:10:30.309
hire, the way that you train, the way that you fire, the way

00:10:30.309 --> 00:10:33.750
that you promote, award, whatever - when you align all that

00:10:33.769 --> 00:10:36.590
it becomes an organizing structure.

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:39.720
It's almost in lieu of a lot of levels of management and it

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.509
makes you much more nimble because what you're doing is you'

00:10:42.509 --> 00:10:46.689
re articulating a culture. You're hiring people that agree

00:10:46.709 --> 00:10:51.279
with it and you're able to kind of let them loose. You're

00:10:51.299 --> 00:10:53.289
much more productive. You're much more effective.

00:10:53.289 --> 00:10:57.929
So anyway, this was based on looking at some of the best

00:10:57.949 --> 00:11:00.970
companies and some of the best performing units in those

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:03.679
companies. And they all did the same thing, so it's like "We

00:11:03.679 --> 00:11:06.179
want to do that". Especially if we're selling this, we've

00:11:06.179 --> 00:11:09.929
got to be able to do it. So, we've spent a lot of time now

00:11:09.929 --> 00:11:12.929
trying to immolate them. And I have to say we're not quite

00:11:12.929 --> 00:11:15.679
as good as some of our customers, but we're trying hard.

00:11:15.679 --> 00:11:18.250
So based on your background, one attribute or quality of

00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:21.889
your company - and given what you do - I could imagine it

00:11:21.909 --> 00:11:30.460
would analytical. You know, with BCG in your background and

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.470
- I almost said Morgan Stanley - Goldman Sachs. So, is that

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:35.559
part of your culture - that you value that kind of political

00:11:35.559 --> 00:11:36.559
lens?

00:11:36.559 --> 00:11:39.089
And then the other thing I hear you talk about with your

00:11:39.089 --> 00:11:42.629
clients is sort of valuing of the high performers or the

00:11:42.649 --> 00:11:46.679
elites. Are either of those things that you think are

00:11:46.699 --> 00:11:47.759
distinctive about your...?

00:11:47.779 --> 00:11:50.960
I think there are a couple of things that are really

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:53.730
distinctive about us. One is we truly, truly do not hire

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:57.340
assholes and we will fire them. Pardon my...I mean, we will

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:01.970
let go of high performers who are corrosive. I mean, I

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:09.069
believe very strongly in the sort of dominance of a team vs.

00:12:09.089 --> 00:12:11.149
individual superstars.

00:12:11.169 --> 00:12:16.879
I'd say the other one is our mantra is "no mediocrity". No

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:19.339
mediocrity for our customers. We want to help them become

00:12:19.339 --> 00:12:23.939
great companies. No mediocrity for our team. So, we're this

00:12:23.959 --> 00:12:27.389
interesting mix of super high performance, but a really nice

00:12:27.409 --> 00:12:28.490
group of people.

00:12:28.509 --> 00:12:29.509
Right.

00:12:29.509 --> 00:12:31.370
And so, I think that for the right person that really

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:34.209
resonates. For a lot of people it doesn't and they don't

00:12:34.209 --> 00:12:37.459
want to have anything to do with us. But for the right group

00:12:37.459 --> 00:12:40.459
of people they feel like, "Well, this is a great place. I

00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:42.459
can really strive and yet I feel like everybody's got my

00:12:42.459 --> 00:12:43.459
back.:

00:12:43.459 --> 00:12:46.209
Yeah. Sounds a lot like the Stanford MBA class. And you

00:12:46.209 --> 00:12:48.459
know, I think the faculty certainly are influential in

00:12:48.459 --> 00:12:49.459
supporting the nice people...

00:12:49.459 --> 00:12:50.459
Right.

00:12:50.459 --> 00:12:52.459
that are really, really bright. So, that was a joke that

00:12:52.459 --> 00:12:54.709
didn't quite land. Okay. Jose, do you want to?

00:12:54.709 --> 00:12:58.009
Sure. Jose Roman. I'm the CEO and Founder of a company

00:12:58.029 --> 00:13:01.679
called Jawbone. For those of you that don't know us, we are

00:13:01.699 --> 00:13:04.210
- I think according to our friends at Goldman Sachs - now

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.860
the largest privately held venture backed electronics

00:13:06.879 --> 00:13:09.529
company in the world, very quietly. We're backed by

00:13:09.549 --> 00:13:12.860
Sequoyah, Coastal Ventures, and Andreessen-Horowitz.

00:13:12.879 --> 00:13:15.980
And you know, we've had a long, interesting ride. I was

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.500
actually an undergrad here in the Engineering School back in

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:21.000
the mid '90's when we started the company. We started

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.139
working on ideas in '97 - '98 and really formed the company

00:13:23.139 --> 00:13:24.139
in '99.

00:13:24.139 --> 00:13:27.529
And culture's been an interesting for us because we've had

00:13:27.549 --> 00:13:30.970
many sorts of ups and downs to the point where, you know, at

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:34.000
one point in 2005 the doors were actually chained and we

00:13:34.019 --> 00:13:36.679
were nearly out of business. And we sort of bounced back

00:13:36.679 --> 00:13:38.179
from that and continued to grow.

00:13:38.179 --> 00:13:41.679
So, I would say that a big part of our culture has been sort

00:13:41.679 --> 00:13:44.919
of this perseverance, total belief in the mission and what

00:13:44.939 --> 00:13:47.689
we're trying to accomplish. We like to think of ourselves as

00:13:47.689 --> 00:13:50.399
being in the taste business as well. So, design and being

00:13:50.399 --> 00:13:54.159
design driven is a huge part of that. So, I'd say it's our

00:13:54.179 --> 00:13:57.269
perseverance, design driven and making hard decisions that

00:13:57.289 --> 00:14:00.850
often don't feel like they're the exact right financial

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:01.870
decision.

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:05.480
I'll give you guys an example. So, we make things. We make

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:08.500
lots of things in China. And you know, we use medical grade

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:11.000
plastics in all our products. They cost six times more than

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:14.519
a lot of your typical electronics products do. So, to an

00:14:14.539 --> 00:14:17.000
Asian manufacturer that is an insane decision, right? They

00:14:17.019 --> 00:14:20.919
think we are totally bat shit crazy - sorry for my language.

00:14:20.939 --> 00:14:24.439
And it is a fight. And we have to say, "No, you've got to do

00:14:24.439 --> 00:14:27.439
this. You've got to do that. Get rid of the draft angles."

00:14:27.439 --> 00:14:30.689
And we focus on that. And we ended up winning "Design of the

00:14:30.689 --> 00:14:33.189
Decade" from the IDSA for those decisions. And people value

00:14:33.189 --> 00:14:35.939
that and it's why we can sell products at higher premiums,

00:14:35.939 --> 00:14:38.100
better gross margins. We avoid the commoditization cycle

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:39.870
that you see with a lot of other consumer electronics

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:40.870
products.

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:43.620
So, it all sort of comes together - the sort of perseverance

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.159
and fighting for hard decisions, as well as, you know,

00:14:46.179 --> 00:14:49.000
staying true to the value, and having great taste

00:14:49.019 --> 00:14:54.389
experience, and things like that. So...

00:14:54.409 --> 00:14:55.409
Alright. Andrew?

00:14:55.409 --> 00:14:59.350
Andrew Hauser. I am currently Founder and CEO of Freedom

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:01.779
Financial Network and Bills.com which are two sister

00:15:01.799 --> 00:15:06.149
companies that help people with financial challenges. It

00:15:06.169 --> 00:15:09.850
started in 2003 shortly after I graduated from the GSB in

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:16.459
2002 with my friend and Co - Founder, Brad Stroh. And we've

00:15:16.479 --> 00:15:20.340
grown from a spare bedroom to about 550 - close to 600

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:21.360
employees today.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:24.509
And I've been able to watch the evolution of the culture -

00:15:24.529 --> 00:15:28.620
the creation of the culture - from the very start. And I

00:15:28.639 --> 00:15:33.120
think just listening to your intro, you know, you talked

00:15:33.139 --> 00:15:37.959
about what is unique or what is rare. And what's interesting

00:15:37.979 --> 00:15:40.089
is thinking about that. There's nothing that's really unique

00:15:40.089 --> 00:15:43.279
and rare in theory, right? I could go cut and paste someone'

00:15:43.299 --> 00:15:46.299
s mission, vision and values, and put them on my website and

00:15:46.309 --> 00:15:49.829
call them my own. And that wouldn't do me a whole lot of

00:15:49.849 --> 00:15:50.849
good.

00:15:50.849 --> 00:15:55.850
What's unique and rare is living it and believing it. That's

00:15:55.870 --> 00:16:00.740
hard. I can take any seven values in the world, but if I

00:16:00.759 --> 00:16:03.759
don't believe in them and I don't live them every day, then

00:16:03.759 --> 00:16:07.259
that's not really going to do me a whole lot of good. And I

00:16:07.259 --> 00:16:11.389
think that's the challenge for any entrepreneur. It's

00:16:11.409 --> 00:16:15.370
constant. We talk about constancy, consistency every single

00:16:15.389 --> 00:16:18.759
day. You have to be living those values. You have to be

00:16:18.779 --> 00:16:23.600
living what you tell your employees you care about. And you

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:24.620
can never be off.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:29.189
You know, they say a cook is judged by their worst meal. I

00:16:29.209 --> 00:16:31.490
like to say, " A leader is judged by their worst

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:35.049
interaction." You know, you walk in every day, and you live

00:16:35.069 --> 00:16:38.009
your values, and you say what you need to say about your

00:16:38.009 --> 00:16:41.879
customers, about your employees. And then on the 201st day

00:16:41.899 --> 00:16:44.399
you walk in and make some offhand comment about your

00:16:44.399 --> 00:16:46.649
employees or about your customers, that's the comment people

00:16:46.649 --> 00:16:49.929
are going to remember and that one comment can undermine

00:16:49.949 --> 00:16:52.569
years of work.

00:16:52.589 --> 00:16:54.870
So, we talk about responsibility. We talk about

00:16:54.889 --> 00:16:56.709
accountability. We talk about getting good people in the

00:16:56.709 --> 00:17:00.500
door, getting bad people out of the door. But if you just

00:17:00.519 --> 00:17:03.479
talk about it and you don't actually do it - you don't give

00:17:03.479 --> 00:17:05.230
people the responsibility, you don't hold people

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:07.960
accountable, you don't get the - I won't use the word. I'll

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:13.839
be the only person on the panel that doesn't swear in the

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:16.000
intro.

00:17:16.019 --> 00:17:18.549
[Informal talk] But if you don't live that, then you don't

00:17:18.549 --> 00:17:20.210
have a culture. So...

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:23.480
So, let me ask this question. I mean, I think on this issue

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:27.109
of living it - what are the things - the specific behaviors,

00:17:27.129 --> 00:17:31.019
routines - things that you do on a regular basis to

00:17:31.039 --> 00:17:36.149
reinforce and to create that culture. I mean, so what does

00:17:36.169 --> 00:17:39.169
it actually look like? If we were to follow you around with

00:17:39.169 --> 00:17:42.720
a camera for, you know, a month what would be the kinds of

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:46.690
interactions, events, things that really reflect where the

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:49.720
culture is created in your organization? Anybody.

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:55.670
[inaudible] Well, I was thinking about there's informal ways

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:58.710
and there's formal ways. And you know, informally - for

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:01.240
example, we have an office in Phoenix with about 350

00:18:01.259 --> 00:18:04.970
employees. I go there once a month for a few days - a week,

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:08.240
a month. And when I walk in I walk around the entire office.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:10.930
It's now enormous. It's 80,000 square feet. And I say hi to

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:13.430
everyone and try to shake as many hands as I can when I

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:14.430
first walk in.

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:19.220
It seems like a relatively basic thing, but I've gotten so

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:21.720
much feedback - both direct and indirect - on how much it

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:24.440
means to these people to have me walk in and just ask them

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:27.779
how things are going. It takes me, you know, maybe an hour

00:18:27.799 --> 00:18:30.799
that first day that I'm in the office - an hour - two hours

00:18:30.799 --> 00:18:33.769
- just to walk around to all 350 people, say "hi", say "Good

00:18:33.769 --> 00:18:38.859
morning. What's the latest?" Those are informal things.

00:18:38.879 --> 00:18:43.490
Informally, just the decisions you make to get rid of

00:18:43.509 --> 00:18:45.569
people, the communications you have.

00:18:45.589 --> 00:18:48.589
But then there's the formal things. We stole a page out of

00:18:48.589 --> 00:18:53.430
Stanford's book. We have brown bag lunches every month where

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:56.399
I get in a room about this size and anyone that wants to

00:18:56.399 --> 00:18:58.980
bring their lunch comes by and asks me whatever question

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:00.000
they want.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.909
We have a "Founder's Corner" on the internet. Every day we

00:19:03.929 --> 00:19:08.799
update it with news and things that, you know, back up those

00:19:08.819 --> 00:19:13.480
values and our vision that we're trying to create. So, both

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:16.329
formally and informally your actions every day and every

00:19:16.349 --> 00:19:17.549
week help support that.

00:19:17.569 --> 00:19:22.549
I think we're going through a process right now where we're

00:19:22.569 --> 00:19:25.529
just over 100 people. We're 110 people and we need to be

00:19:25.529 --> 00:19:29.289
much more proactive about our culture. So, what we've done

00:19:29.309 --> 00:19:33.450
is we took our corporate vision - which we worked out about

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:36.720
a year ago - and then we worked out all our values. We kind

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:39.529
of refreshed the values with a new crop of people.

00:19:39.549 --> 00:19:42.730
And then, we go to each department head and we say, "What is

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:45.649
your department head mission?" And then make sure that that'

00:19:45.669 --> 00:19:48.399
s relating to the corporate mission. And then, "What are

00:19:48.419 --> 00:19:52.789
your metrics? What does this look like at the end of the

00:19:52.809 --> 00:19:55.559
year and how do you know that you've succeeded? Once, you

00:19:55.559 --> 00:19:58.559
know, you've hit it out of the ballpark, what is a single?"

00:19:58.559 --> 00:20:01.639
And then we meet. In some cases we meet monthly and in some

00:20:01.639 --> 00:20:06.519
cases we meet, you know, every quarter and we just see, you

00:20:06.539 --> 00:20:09.539
know, "Are you on track?" If you're not on track, "Is there

00:20:09.539 --> 00:20:11.789
a problem or do we have the wrong metrics?"

00:20:11.789 --> 00:20:15.869
But you're getting everybody to define in really, really

00:20:15.889 --> 00:20:19.649
concrete terms what their role is specifically in supporting

00:20:19.669 --> 00:20:23.129
that overall mission. And then you are giving them a way to

00:20:23.149 --> 00:20:26.740
see how they're doing, and feel accountable for it. And so,

00:20:26.759 --> 00:20:31.599
it kind of makes the whole organization come together.

00:20:31.619 --> 00:20:38.149
I always find these conversations a little bit theoretical.

00:20:38.169 --> 00:20:41.490
So, one of my favorite examples is the best Gold's Gym. And

00:20:41.509 --> 00:20:45.490
the Gold's Gym system has to be in the middle of Washington

00:20:45.509 --> 00:20:50.289
State. The guy who runs it, you know, their mission is to

00:20:50.309 --> 00:20:52.839
make their community - the Wenatchee Valley - the fittest

00:20:52.859 --> 00:20:58.200
community in America. And he's given each department, you

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:01.539
know, a role and he says things. To the front desk people he

00:21:01.559 --> 00:21:05.740
says, "When you pick up the phone in less than two rings you

00:21:05.759 --> 00:21:08.859
are likely helping somebody to be able to play with their

00:21:08.879 --> 00:21:11.079
grandchildren in their seventies."

00:21:11.099 --> 00:21:16.170
And that takes a very kind of theoretical, you know,

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:18.440
fitness, blah, blah, blah. And it's making it really

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:21.490
concrete. It's making it visual. It's making it a powerful

00:21:21.509 --> 00:21:24.240
story and suddenly it's not about answering the phone. Whoa!

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:27.420
It's about helping this person take this first step towards

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:29.509
fitness and playing with their grandchildren.

00:21:29.529 --> 00:21:32.569
And a couple of other things he does - I don't know if you

00:21:32.569 --> 00:21:35.259
guys know anything about the fitness industry. But some of

00:21:35.279 --> 00:21:38.769
their sacred cows are, you know, they want to lock you in.

00:21:38.789 --> 00:21:41.789
They want you to pay a high initiation fee. They want to

00:21:41.789 --> 00:21:44.539
lock you in with the longest contract possible and they want

00:21:44.539 --> 00:21:47.539
to make it really painful for you to get out of that

00:21:47.539 --> 00:21:50.539
contract. So, if you move you have to fax in your new

00:21:50.539 --> 00:21:53.039
residence papers, or your new lease papers, or your new...

00:21:53.039 --> 00:21:55.649
They make it really, really hard. This guy said, "That's

00:21:55.649 --> 00:21:59.619
serving our mission of making this the fittest community in

00:21:59.639 --> 00:22:04.109
America. No. What would it take for us to get the results

00:22:04.129 --> 00:22:06.509
that we need - the retention that we need - without a lockup

00:22:06.509 --> 00:22:09.950
and without high initiation fees?" And they got rid of them.

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:12.970
I mean, this is unheard of in the fitness industry. They are

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:15.720
still one of the highest performing gyms. And I think when

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:19.220
you walk in the door of that gym as a customer or as a

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:21.720
prospective customer you feel that these people want to help

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:24.799
you on your journey and they're not just kind of cleaning up

00:22:24.799 --> 00:22:27.379
the mission. That is a totally different experience from

00:22:27.399 --> 00:22:28.399
your average gym.

00:22:28.399 --> 00:22:31.069
So, I mean this is a really concrete example of what it

00:22:31.069 --> 00:22:35.879
means to have that culture. And he will fire people. He's

00:22:35.899 --> 00:22:38.399
not interested in people necessarily having super toned

00:22:38.419 --> 00:22:41.109
bodies, although he himself has one. He wants people that

00:22:41.129 --> 00:22:43.629
care very much about other people, convey that and really

00:22:43.629 --> 00:22:46.409
buy into that mission. It's extraordinarily powerful. So,

00:22:46.429 --> 00:22:48.689
that's just a concrete example that I find very inspiring.

00:22:48.689 --> 00:22:53.759
Yeah, I mean I kind of bet that it's pretty hard to get that

00:22:53.779 --> 00:22:56.029
attitude in those practices to diffuse throughout the other

00:22:56.039 --> 00:22:57.039
Gold's Gyms.

00:22:57.039 --> 00:22:58.619
We're working on it.

00:22:58.639 --> 00:22:59.639
Yeah.

00:22:59.639 --> 00:23:00.639
But yes it is.

00:23:00.639 --> 00:23:02.419
See?

00:23:02.439 --> 00:23:07.329
You know, it's interesting. In my experience, this is the

00:23:07.349 --> 00:23:10.099
only job I've ever had and we interact with all different

00:23:10.099 --> 00:23:12.970
kinds of companies - big ones like AT&amp;T and smaller ones

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:15.539
that are application partners of ours and things like that -

00:23:15.559 --> 00:23:18.149
startups with five people to

00:23:18.169 --> 00:23:21.159
[Inaudible] Telecom which has 250,000 people. And you see

00:23:21.179 --> 00:23:22.519
sort of a wide range of cultures.

00:23:22.519 --> 00:23:26.159
And I think that the thing that I've realized is that each

00:23:26.179 --> 00:23:28.879
culture and each company has to be appropriate for that

00:23:28.899 --> 00:23:33.099
company. So, it's really hard to take something that you see

00:23:33.119 --> 00:23:35.869
as a best practice somewhere else and try to impose that

00:23:35.869 --> 00:23:39.009
into your organization. We do this a lot too. It was

00:23:39.029 --> 00:23:41.779
interesting to hear you talk about, you know, lots of people

00:23:41.779 --> 00:23:44.529
coming in from the outside. Obviously as you grow and you

00:23:44.529 --> 00:23:47.279
scale you hire people who've had jobs at other places. You

00:23:47.279 --> 00:23:49.029
can't grow everyone from the internal organization.

00:23:49.029 --> 00:23:52.279
So, the first thing I tell all the execs I hired is, "Forget

00:23:52.279 --> 00:23:54.529
everything you learned in all those other places." Your

00:23:54.529 --> 00:23:57.029
experience is about helping us not make the same mistakes,

00:23:57.029 --> 00:23:59.779
but it's a totally new playbook. And let's not even think

00:23:59.779 --> 00:24:03.279
about the things that we did in the past. We have to sort of

00:24:03.279 --> 00:24:05.779
have the appropriate systems and culture for where we are

00:24:05.779 --> 00:24:08.529
today. That doesn't mean that we don't have values that have

00:24:08.529 --> 00:24:11.779
sort of permeated us for a long time. And the way you live

00:24:11.779 --> 00:24:14.529
it I think changes as the company changes, as the company

00:24:14.529 --> 00:24:16.779
grows. As you get international and you have multiple

00:24:16.779 --> 00:24:19.529
offices in places you have to do different things all the

00:24:19.529 --> 00:24:22.029
way along, and constantly be watching it, and evolving with

00:24:22.029 --> 00:24:24.279
it. Do what's appropriate for your organization because it's

00:24:24.279 --> 00:24:27.029
not going to be the same for different people depending on

00:24:27.029 --> 00:24:28.779
your product, your service, or any of those things. That's

00:24:28.779 --> 00:24:29.779
important.

00:24:29.779 --> 00:24:32.779
So, I'll give you an example from our past. You know, we

00:24:32.779 --> 00:24:35.529
make these headset products and a lot of customers like AT&amp;

00:24:35.529 --> 00:24:38.029
T, they make you put your device into their standardized

00:24:38.029 --> 00:24:41.379
packaging which reduces a lot of the differentiation you get

00:24:41.399 --> 00:24:44.399
or the way you can explain and tell the story around what

00:24:44.399 --> 00:24:47.819
you're doing. And it was a huge fight for us because we had

00:24:47.819 --> 00:24:50.569
worked it into contracts that we would, you know, get our

00:24:50.569 --> 00:24:53.319
own packaging and be able to do our special thing at the

00:24:53.319 --> 00:24:54.319
point of sale.

00:24:54.319 --> 00:24:57.069
And there was a regime change, you know, merger and things

00:24:57.069 --> 00:24:59.819
like that. This was in the Cingular days. They became AT&amp;T

00:24:59.819 --> 00:25:02.819
and all those contracts went out the window. And there was a

00:25:02.819 --> 00:25:04.819
period of time where we didn't actually have

00:25:04.819 --> 00:25:07.319
[inaudible] on the shelf because we refused to sell. It was

00:25:07.319 --> 00:25:09.819
our biggest customer and they were trying to impose their

00:25:09.819 --> 00:25:13.069
will in terms of making us fit a certain way and do a

00:25:13.069 --> 00:25:16.069
certain thing. And we just said, "No, we're not going to do

00:25:16.069 --> 00:25:18.819
it. And therefore, we're not going to sell your product on

00:25:18.819 --> 00:25:21.569
our shelves." And that was a hard decision. It was our

00:25:21.569 --> 00:25:24.069
biggest customer. You can imagine what the venture guys felt

00:25:24.069 --> 00:25:26.569
like at a board level. They thought we were crazy.

00:25:26.569 --> 00:25:30.069
But we sort of had a long - term view and we said, "You know

00:25:30.069 --> 00:25:33.069
what? If you, again, allow this to happen and don't give us

00:25:33.069 --> 00:25:36.319
the ability to be true to what our values are and what we

00:25:36.319 --> 00:25:39.069
want our brand to stand for, then it's a slippery slope,

00:25:39.069 --> 00:25:41.819
alright?" You've got to have people on your team, on your

00:25:41.819 --> 00:25:45.019
board that are willing to go with you on that. That's hard.

00:25:45.019 --> 00:25:50.269
So, you want to think about, you know, what are the daily

00:25:50.289 --> 00:25:53.299
interactions that you have that reinforce or describe the

00:25:53.319 --> 00:25:56.700
culture of the company and kind of break it down in two ways

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:59.470
- one is, you know, as an individual, as a CEO - "What

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:01.970
interactions that you have and what are the messages that

00:26:01.970 --> 00:26:04.970
you give off? But let me start first with, "What are the

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:08.049
processes? What are the things that we do as a company that

00:26:08.069 --> 00:26:10.559
everybody that comes through the company experiences in a

00:26:10.579 --> 00:26:12.349
very consistent way?"

00:26:12.369 --> 00:26:15.700
So, first off is, you know, we did spend time like everybody

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:18.470
I guess sitting down and deciding, "What are the core values

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:21.529
that describe us - that describe the founders, describe the

00:26:21.549 --> 00:26:24.319
company we want to be, describe the relationships we want to

00:26:24.339 --> 00:26:27.089
have with our customers?" We spent a lot of time trying to

00:26:27.089 --> 00:26:28.089
think about this.

00:26:28.089 --> 00:26:31.460
When we started to hire, we tried to convert those core

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:36.059
values into behavioral characteristics that we could use to

00:26:36.079 --> 00:26:38.970
select the right people. So, when looking at a group of very

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:41.329
talented people, how do you select the people that

00:26:41.349 --> 00:26:43.569
culturally are going to be the best fit for you?

00:26:43.569 --> 00:26:46.869
So, one of the things we do is everybody that's in the

00:26:46.889 --> 00:26:49.139
company we train in a standard methodology for recruiting.

00:26:49.139 --> 00:26:51.579
We use a behavioral interviewing process. Everybody learns

00:26:51.599 --> 00:26:55.029
the language, everybody becomes accustomed to it. And every

00:26:55.049 --> 00:26:57.490
single person that comes into the company goes through a

00:26:57.490 --> 00:27:01.190
rigorous, rigorous recruiting process where, you know,

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:04.809
everybody has a role in the process. It's divvied up across

00:27:04.829 --> 00:27:06.599
an entire interview team.

00:27:06.619 --> 00:27:10.379
And then, when we finish the process, when we come back to

00:27:10.399 --> 00:27:13.549
talk about that particular candidate we do it as a team. And

00:27:13.569 --> 00:27:17.019
we literally call it Gun Head. The decision at the end of

00:27:17.039 --> 00:27:21.049
the meeting is Gun Head. Do you say hire or do you say not

00:27:21.069 --> 00:27:22.849
hire. And it's a unanimous decision.

00:27:22.869 --> 00:27:26.049
So, everybody takes responsibility for people that come into

00:27:26.069 --> 00:27:29.069
the company because they played a direct role in it. And if

00:27:29.069 --> 00:27:31.990
the process is hard, what you find is that even though I may

00:27:31.990 --> 00:27:35.240
not know you - you may work in a different office than I do

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:37.990
- if I know that you went through the exact same rigorous

00:27:37.990 --> 00:27:40.470
process I feel confident that you're good because you went

00:27:40.470 --> 00:27:42.740
through the same filter that I did.

00:27:42.759 --> 00:27:45.899
So, once the employees come in, the other thing we talk

00:27:45.919 --> 00:27:50.809
about is wanting to be able to evaluate and communicate how

00:27:50.829 --> 00:27:55.389
people are doing. So, we're also less so in the phase of

00:27:55.409 --> 00:27:57.819
this new startup company, but in the operations Phase and

00:27:57.819 --> 00:28:02.240
Fiber Tower, we were very rigorous about defining the PP &amp; D

00:28:02.259 --> 00:28:05.389
- the performance evaluation structure we would use for the

00:28:05.409 --> 00:28:08.859
people in the company. And some of it was company related

00:28:08.879 --> 00:28:11.329
objectives, some of it was personal related objectives. And

00:28:11.349 --> 00:28:13.949
most of the personal objectives tied back to the core values

00:28:13.949 --> 00:28:18.129
of the company. But we would tie compensation and even more

00:28:18.149 --> 00:28:20.940
importantly we would tie equity to the high performers in

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:21.960
the company.

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:24.980
So, one of the processes we used to go through is we would

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:27.230
stack rank employees in the company and we would

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:31.329
disproportionately divvy up equity at the end of every year.

00:28:31.349 --> 00:28:34.839
So, my goal was to take 100% of equity and give 80% of it to

00:28:34.839 --> 00:28:38.849
the "A" players. And that just reinforced a culture of

00:28:38.869 --> 00:28:42.629
people that were trying to exceed their own capabilities,

00:28:42.649 --> 00:28:45.159
and then also having a very humane way for people that aren'

00:28:45.159 --> 00:28:49.109
t working in the company to exit them out of the company in

00:28:49.129 --> 00:28:51.490
a respectful and you know, reasonable process where they

00:28:51.509 --> 00:28:53.519
just can't be part of your organization.

00:28:53.539 --> 00:28:56.039
And then, you know, things I talked about earlier like

00:28:56.039 --> 00:28:59.450
feedback, being able to address issues in a very

00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:02.279
straightforward way. Culturally for us that was a big deal.

00:29:02.299 --> 00:29:06.029
So, you would often see people leave a meeting where it was

00:29:06.049 --> 00:29:08.799
a very simple meeting, but you know, hard decisions need to

00:29:08.799 --> 00:29:11.549
be made. And they might grab somebody in the meeting and

00:29:11.549 --> 00:29:14.549
say, you know, "I understand what you were trying to do, but

00:29:14.549 --> 00:29:16.549
this behavior that you demonstrated was inconsistent with

00:29:16.549 --> 00:29:19.549
that goal." And you may not have realized it at the time,

00:29:19.549 --> 00:29:23.049
but if you take it in the vein that person is there to help

00:29:23.049 --> 00:29:25.049
you be better at what you do, it's a very productive

00:29:25.049 --> 00:29:26.049
experience.

00:29:26.049 --> 00:29:30.669
And then, I guess finally, I think it's the individual

00:29:30.689 --> 00:29:33.789
aspect. "What do you do as an individual in a company?" So,

00:29:33.809 --> 00:29:36.809
I guess similar to Andrew we had 14 offices. I used to

00:29:36.809 --> 00:29:39.639
travel to every office on a regular circuit. I would go

00:29:39.659 --> 00:29:42.539
visit all 14 different offices in different states. And

00:29:42.559 --> 00:29:45.559
while I was there I'd try to pick a couple of different

00:29:45.559 --> 00:29:48.559
positions there, and I'd spend a day with them. So, if it

00:29:48.559 --> 00:29:52.059
was a Field Tech I'd spend a day in the field with them. If

00:29:52.059 --> 00:29:55.059
it was, you know, one of our Account Manager's I might go

00:29:55.059 --> 00:29:58.059
visit some of the customers. But I wanted to make sure that

00:29:58.059 --> 00:30:01.059
I knew every single role in the company and what they did.

00:30:01.059 --> 00:30:04.309
And they knew that I knew that. So, when I was talking about

00:30:04.309 --> 00:30:07.309
that or when I was experiencing them, you know, in terms of

00:30:07.309 --> 00:30:09.559
evaluating them I had a greater feel for it.

00:30:09.559 --> 00:30:12.309
And then, the final thing that we did was it's very

00:30:12.309 --> 00:30:15.059
difficult at the top of a company to recognize the really

00:30:15.059 --> 00:30:17.809
amazing things that happen. And what you find is there are

00:30:17.809 --> 00:30:20.309
heroic things that happen every day that never really get

00:30:20.309 --> 00:30:25.409
talked about. So, we implemented a sort of an employee based

00:30:25.429 --> 00:30:28.419
recognition process we call "Shout Out's" where any employee

00:30:28.439 --> 00:30:31.750
in the company could give any other person a recognition

00:30:31.769 --> 00:30:36.119
award and that person would get a $50 gift certificate. But

00:30:36.139 --> 00:30:38.730
the requirement to do it is they had to draft an email to

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:42.710
the entire company and describe what happened. And we didn't

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:45.240
make that up. We stole that from another company, but...

00:30:45.259 --> 00:30:46.259
That's cool.

00:30:46.259 --> 00:30:49.299
But it was great because people had the opportunity to hear

00:30:49.319 --> 00:30:51.919
- even, you know, at different levels of the company -

00:30:51.939 --> 00:30:54.599
amazing and heroic things that were happening.

00:30:54.619 --> 00:30:58.769
And then the final thing was creating transparency. And we

00:30:58.789 --> 00:31:01.970
tried to over communicate to the company. So, we had a

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:04.849
policy that after every board meeting I would hold a

00:31:04.869 --> 00:31:07.539
companywide communication and I would share what was

00:31:07.559 --> 00:31:10.849
described. Not all of it was always positive, but at least

00:31:10.869 --> 00:31:13.709
they knew exactly where we stood. So, I never viewed my role

00:31:13.709 --> 00:31:16.319
as a marketing role to the employees in the company. I

00:31:16.319 --> 00:31:20.349
viewed myself as sort of providing the current direction of

00:31:20.369 --> 00:31:22.889
what we were doing and what was important for us to be

00:31:22.889 --> 00:31:24.619
successful. So, that was

00:31:24.639 --> 00:31:25.639
[informal talk].

00:31:25.639 --> 00:31:29.919
So, let me just back up on this feedback. And again, one of

00:31:29.939 --> 00:31:33.240
the things you're known for is your credibility in the

00:31:33.259 --> 00:31:38.119
touchy feely realm of high performance leadership. But I

00:31:38.139 --> 00:31:41.259
don't think there's kind of another company here that

00:31:41.279 --> 00:31:45.919
wouldn't say, "We really value giving honest and accurate

00:31:45.939 --> 00:31:52.240
feedback." But yet it's hard to do. What is it that you

00:31:52.259 --> 00:31:56.819
found to be kind of most challenging about really living

00:31:56.839 --> 00:32:04.019
consistently that value of being honest and constructive in

00:32:04.039 --> 00:32:08.869
providing feedback? I mean, and why is it so rare?

00:32:08.889 --> 00:32:12.139
First off, the ironic theme is I'm the only one on the team

00:32:12.139 --> 00:32:14.889
that didn't actually take the class. So, I actually took it

00:32:14.889 --> 00:32:17.639
through Exec Ed to go figure out what everybody was talking

00:32:17.639 --> 00:32:18.990
about.

00:32:19.009 --> 00:32:22.929
It's a process that requires continuity. It's a process that

00:32:22.949 --> 00:32:28.089
if you do it once it can be very destructive. So, we always

00:32:28.109 --> 00:32:31.599
looked at it as a method to be able to. In doing a startup,

00:32:31.599 --> 00:32:34.469
you never know if you're going to be successful. You know,

00:32:34.469 --> 00:32:36.969
if you look at the odds it's uncanny how many of them

00:32:36.969 --> 00:32:37.969
actually fail.

00:32:37.969 --> 00:32:41.469
So, we viewed it as a process that even if we did the best

00:32:41.469 --> 00:32:44.469
we could and the company didn't work, we wanted to be better

00:32:44.469 --> 00:32:46.289
professionals, better individuals, better executives, better

00:32:46.309 --> 00:32:49.000
whatever it was and we viewed this as a process for

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.509
ourselves. So, in many ways it was very selfish process

00:32:51.529 --> 00:32:54.480
because it was a way for us to get feedback from people that

00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:56.309
we really respected.

00:32:56.329 --> 00:33:00.639
The hardest part was getting people to trust the process.

00:33:00.659 --> 00:33:03.720
So, we grew very quickly. We hired people from a lot of

00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:06.699
large - ironically - wireless carriers. We had the CTO from

00:33:06.719 --> 00:33:10.339
one of the major carriers join us. And we would always give

00:33:10.359 --> 00:33:12.529
them a free ride through the first feedback session.

00:33:12.529 --> 00:33:15.649
So, I do an executive feedback session and inevitably, I

00:33:15.669 --> 00:33:18.059
would talk with them afterwards and they would all go, "That

00:33:18.059 --> 00:33:21.480
was really rough! You guys were tough on each other!" And

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:25.029
you know, it was interesting because that was a symptom of

00:33:25.049 --> 00:33:28.230
the fact that even though everything that needed to be

00:33:28.250 --> 00:33:31.279
discussed was being discussed and it was done in a very

00:33:31.299 --> 00:33:33.549
frank - and maybe too efficient - way, he had never

00:33:33.549 --> 00:33:37.089
experienced that in where he came from. There was always the

00:33:37.109 --> 00:33:40.329
superficial aspect of communication - the 360 evaluation,

00:33:40.349 --> 00:33:42.859
the review that comes from the boss - but not something

00:33:42.879 --> 00:33:43.879
that's very specific.

00:33:43.879 --> 00:33:46.869
So for us, when we did a feedback it was three positives,

00:33:46.869 --> 00:33:50.000
three negatives and every single one that you did had to be

00:33:50.019 --> 00:33:53.569
backed up by a specific example. You had to say, "In this

00:33:53.589 --> 00:33:57.059
situation, you did this and here was the way that I

00:33:57.079 --> 00:34:00.449
perceived your behavior." You can't argue with that. The

00:34:00.469 --> 00:34:03.719
goal was not to argue with that. The goal was to take that

00:34:03.719 --> 00:34:05.969
because that was that person's perspective. You may disagree

00:34:05.969 --> 00:34:08.539
with it, but that was what they felt.

00:34:08.559 --> 00:34:12.599
So, implementing this was training the people how to do it,

00:34:12.619 --> 00:34:15.719
having the continuity that it would happen on a continuous,

00:34:15.739 --> 00:34:19.009
repeatable basis, and then building the trust that it wasn't

00:34:19.029 --> 00:34:25.049
done, you know, as an offense weapon against somebody. But

00:34:25.069 --> 00:34:26.069
the value was for you.

00:34:26.069 --> 00:34:28.630
So for example, for us HR never attended the feedback

00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:31.809
session. Not a single comment that ever was given in

00:34:31.829 --> 00:34:35.690
feedback affected your actual pay or equity in the company.

00:34:35.710 --> 00:34:40.000
It was purely for you. So, it was just a way that we

00:34:40.019 --> 00:34:43.519
interpreted that particular technique.

00:34:43.539 --> 00:34:48.269
Okay. Thanks. So, let me ask Andrew a different question. I

00:34:48.289 --> 00:34:51.769
sort of thought about some questions based on the particular

00:34:51.789 --> 00:34:56.259
challenges that the organizations might face. How do you

00:34:56.279 --> 00:35:01.799
think about or deal with the clash between your company's

00:35:01.819 --> 00:35:05.639
culture and how you think about what you're doing, and then

00:35:05.659 --> 00:35:09.440
maybe the industry values, philosophy, or culture, right?

00:35:09.460 --> 00:35:12.650
So, when Consumer Credit - certainly during a period of time

00:35:12.670 --> 00:35:16.259
it was, you know, "Spend. Spend. Spend.", you know, "Borrow.

00:35:16.279 --> 00:35:20.389
Borrow. Borrow." And to the extent that you're really trying

00:35:20.409 --> 00:35:24.719
to operate differently in that context, how do you protect

00:35:24.739 --> 00:35:30.179
your organization from the philosophy in the industry?

00:35:30.199 --> 00:35:35.949
That's a very fitting question. Not just Consumer Credit in

00:35:35.969 --> 00:35:40.000
general as we all know had some issues over the past several

00:35:40.019 --> 00:35:43.860
years, but our industry in particular when we started the

00:35:43.880 --> 00:35:46.699
company it was an innovative idea, a way of working out

00:35:46.719 --> 00:35:51.750
consumers debts outside of a bankruptcy. We were one of the

00:35:51.769 --> 00:35:54.519
few people doing it. We became the industry leader, but it

00:35:54.519 --> 00:35:56.849
was a classic

00:35:56.869 --> 00:36:02.009
[inaudible] business that resulted in a flood of competitors

00:36:02.029 --> 00:36:05.119
especially after all the mortgage people went out of

00:36:05.139 --> 00:36:06.960
business. They jumped into our industry.

00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:11.139
So, there became a very big disconnect between what we were

00:36:11.159 --> 00:36:14.900
saying we were doing for customers, and our core values, and

00:36:14.920 --> 00:36:18.710
we belief that we are this white knight here to help our

00:36:18.730 --> 00:36:21.579
consumers, and we'll do anything to help our customers and

00:36:21.599 --> 00:36:25.929
it's a very emotional, high touch product, and the rest of

00:36:25.949 --> 00:36:28.840
the industry - not all of the industry, but certainly what

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:35.670
got picked up in the press. A very, very big disconnect. It

00:36:35.690 --> 00:36:39.409
actually - I don't want to go into too many details, but

00:36:39.429 --> 00:36:41.960
because we were the industry leader and the industry started

00:36:41.980 --> 00:36:44.730
to get a bad name we wound up getting sued by the State of

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:45.730
California.

00:36:45.730 --> 00:36:48.480
And so, here we are having built this culture around belief

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:51.230
in our employees, belief in what we're doing, taking care of

00:36:51.230 --> 00:36:54.059
customers, helping people through this difficult emotional

00:36:54.079 --> 00:36:57.599
point in their life. And all the sudden the State of

00:36:57.619 --> 00:37:00.610
California is suing us and saying we're taking advantage of

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:04.579
consumers. You know, that's two pretty big different things.

00:37:04.599 --> 00:37:08.089
We're helping consumers through this emotional period. We're

00:37:08.109 --> 00:37:10.299
taking advantage of consumers is what the State of

00:37:10.299 --> 00:37:11.299
California said.

00:37:11.299 --> 00:37:14.739
So, how do we deal with that? It's difficult, but

00:37:14.759 --> 00:37:18.989
communication is extraordinarily important. You can't spin

00:37:19.009 --> 00:37:22.449
that, right? It's in the news, it's on TV, it's on blogs.

00:37:22.449 --> 00:37:25.829
Your competitors are selling against it every day. So, you'

00:37:25.849 --> 00:37:29.469
re not spinning it. You're being open, and honest, and you'

00:37:29.489 --> 00:37:33.309
re communicating every day, every week. It was an emotional

00:37:33.329 --> 00:37:38.900
period for us. But I think, you know, part of it is your

00:37:38.920 --> 00:37:41.420
belief which is contagious. True belief in what you're doing

00:37:41.420 --> 00:37:45.289
is contagious, right? If people see that you're really

00:37:45.309 --> 00:37:48.250
passionate about what you're doing, if you believe in what

00:37:48.269 --> 00:37:50.269
you're doing, then they're going to believe it.

00:37:50.269 --> 00:37:53.269
And it starts with the founders, but then it goes to, you

00:37:53.269 --> 00:37:55.849
know, the next level down. That's really, really important

00:37:55.869 --> 00:37:58.500
is getting people in the door early as an entrepreneur.

00:37:58.519 --> 00:38:01.139
Those are your ambassadors that are going to carry your

00:38:01.159 --> 00:38:06.929
message to the rest of the company. And you know, how do you

00:38:06.949 --> 00:38:10.480
get good people as a startup? You know, that's a crucial

00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:14.279
question because it's going to pay dividends for tough

00:38:14.299 --> 00:38:18.569
events like this. So, communication, consistency, honesty

00:38:18.589 --> 00:38:20.889
and then having a really strong management team that truly

00:38:20.889 --> 00:38:22.329
believes in what they're doing.

00:38:22.329 --> 00:38:25.339
Am I correct in my recollection of the case that it was

00:38:25.359 --> 00:38:26.900
written about

00:38:26.920 --> 00:38:30.559
[inaudible] is that you actually sort of fought this suit,

00:38:30.579 --> 00:38:34.829
you know, longer and harder than some of your advisors that

00:38:34.849 --> 00:38:39.369
were like, "Oh, just settle it or let it go." And you didn'

00:38:39.389 --> 00:38:41.389
t. You kind of persevered in contesting it?

00:38:41.389 --> 00:38:45.250
Yeah, that's correct. There was a case written which some of

00:38:45.269 --> 00:38:49.239
you may have read in the new ventures or one of the new

00:38:49.259 --> 00:38:52.029
entrepreneurship classes. The key decision at the time was,

00:38:52.049 --> 00:38:54.869
"Okay. We have State of California telling us we're doing

00:38:54.889 --> 00:38:57.389
all these bad things. We have millions of dollars in

00:38:57.389 --> 00:39:00.920
potential litigation expenses coming up. We have the press"

00:39:00.940 --> 00:39:03.869
and when the regulator, you know, this is something they

00:39:03.889 --> 00:39:06.049
don't teach enough about in business school - especially in

00:39:06.049 --> 00:39:08.559
this environment - but regulation is everywhere.

00:39:08.579 --> 00:39:11.719
When a regulator decides they're coming after you it's not

00:39:11.739 --> 00:39:14.579
just a negotiation. It's they're calling the ABC station,

00:39:14.599 --> 00:39:17.269
the NBC station. They're trying to do everything in their

00:39:17.269 --> 00:39:21.769
power to sort of press you into a settlement. And so, we

00:39:21.789 --> 00:39:25.559
have this, you know, decision to make. Do we just settle

00:39:25.579 --> 00:39:28.719
this, pay a lot of money, accept that we did something wrong

00:39:28.739 --> 00:39:32.099
and move on or do we fight this, try to deal with the

00:39:32.119 --> 00:39:36.299
consequences to employ morale, to public relations, to our

00:39:36.319 --> 00:39:38.239
competitors ability to sell against us?

00:39:38.259 --> 00:39:41.900
And we ultimately decided to fight. I mean, this is what we'

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:44.670
ve been telling our managers all along. We are not doing

00:39:44.670 --> 00:39:46.920
anything wrong. We believe in what we're doing. We're

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:48.920
helping people. Sure, there's knuckleheads in our industry,

00:39:48.920 --> 00:39:51.730
but we're doing the right thing and we've ultimately decided

00:39:51.730 --> 00:39:55.190
that if we turn around and just agreed, "Okay. We're doing

00:39:55.210 --> 00:39:57.710
something wrong. We'll get out of the State of California.

00:39:57.710 --> 00:40:00.380
We'll pay all this money in fines" that would send the wrong

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:02.329
message, you know?

00:40:02.349 --> 00:40:05.949
And economically it was a tough decision, but in the long

00:40:05.969 --> 00:40:07.719
run it certainly was the right decision. Yeah.

00:40:07.719 --> 00:40:08.719
Did you win?

00:40:08.719 --> 00:40:11.219
We won. I mean, we settled very favorably I would say.

00:40:11.219 --> 00:40:12.219
Good for you.

00:40:12.219 --> 00:40:14.969
It did not actually go to trial, but the settlement was

00:40:14.969 --> 00:40:18.500
extraordinarily favorable. We didn't have to change any

00:40:18.519 --> 00:40:19.519
aspect of our business...

00:40:19.519 --> 00:40:20.519
Good for you.

00:40:20.519 --> 00:40:27.650
which is unusual in a settlement.

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:28.670
That's cool. Okay. Yeah.

00:40:28.670 --> 00:40:29.920
Can I say one thing about the feedback?

00:40:29.920 --> 00:40:30.920
Sure.

00:40:30.920 --> 00:40:33.420
Cause I can't actually stress enough I mean, we've learned

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:36.420
this lesson the hard way because to get through some of the

00:40:36.420 --> 00:40:39.170
tough times we had we built deep friendships with people in

00:40:39.170 --> 00:40:41.670
the organization. And as you scale quickly some of those

00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:44.670
people don't scale with you and yet you're close to them and

00:40:44.670 --> 00:40:47.670
their friends. And the thing that I learned the hard way is

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:49.920
that if you don't give people straight, direct, complete

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:52.670
feedback it is much worse than if you thought you could

00:40:52.670 --> 00:40:55.170
manage it and sort of package it in a way.

00:40:55.170 --> 00:40:58.670
And the thing that we do I think well now that we never did

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:01.670
before - not quite as organized in the way that you guys do

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:04.420
- but we tell people, "Look, this feedback is coming to you

00:41:04.420 --> 00:41:07.420
for one reason and one reason only which is that it's to

00:41:07.420 --> 00:41:10.170
make you better so that we can exceed as an organization."

00:41:10.170 --> 00:41:12.670
It's the only reason you're given feedback because if you're

00:41:12.670 --> 00:41:15.420
not given the feedback it means we don't care anymore and

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:16.670
that's a bad problem, right?

00:41:16.670 --> 00:41:19.420
So, I think if you start those conversations with that sort

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:22.170
of mindset that the reason we're doing this is it's an

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:24.920
investment and if you don't understand it, it's sort of a

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:28.000
way of working through that. People appreciate that,

00:41:28.019 --> 00:41:31.039
alright? Who doesn't want to sort of look in the mirror and

00:41:31.039 --> 00:41:33.039
be able to understand? And sometimes folks just don't do it

00:41:33.039 --> 00:41:34.039
well.

00:41:34.039 --> 00:41:35.039
Right.

00:41:35.039 --> 00:41:37.369
So, I think establishing that right up front that it is

00:41:37.389 --> 00:41:39.869
about being better and about elevating everyone.

00:41:39.889 --> 00:41:43.639
Right. So, let me follow - up on that and actually ask Amy a

00:41:43.659 --> 00:41:46.449
question cause you're in the business - in part - of helping

00:41:46.469 --> 00:41:52.250
your clients to use feedback from customers to make them

00:41:52.269 --> 00:41:58.349
better. And so, how do you influence the culture of your

00:41:58.369 --> 00:42:02.170
clients so that they're really using the data in the way

00:42:02.190 --> 00:42:05.269
that they should be and not biasing it? And I'll tell a

00:42:05.269 --> 00:42:09.210
little anecdote of my experience a number of times with AT&amp;T

00:42:09.230 --> 00:42:11.519
which I don't remember whether they're a client or not. So..

00:42:11.519 --> 00:42:11.719
.

00:42:11.719 --> 00:42:13.319
No.

00:42:13.339 --> 00:42:16.250
So, they were clearly getting lots of feedback and I was on

00:42:16.250 --> 00:42:19.000
the phone three or four times trying to correct, you know,

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:22.000
some issue. And at the end I always got, "Have I answered

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:24.960
all your questions because you're going to be getting a

00:42:24.980 --> 00:42:28.480
survey and I really want you to give me an "A+", or an "H",

00:42:28.480 --> 00:42:31.480
or whatever it was, "When they call you for the survey." And

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:34.480
you know, I certainly felt kind of coerced into that. And I

00:42:34.480 --> 00:42:37.230
go, you know, "If that's what they're doing is that data

00:42:37.230 --> 00:42:39.819
really meaningful?" So, how do you influence the culture in

00:42:39.839 --> 00:42:40.839
your clients?

00:42:40.839 --> 00:42:47.360
So, we try and share best practices. We host a best

00:42:47.380 --> 00:42:50.900
practices conference once a year and bring all of our

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:53.690
clients together. A lot of them get up and give

00:42:53.710 --> 00:42:56.949
presentations. And the example that you gave that is

00:42:56.969 --> 00:43:02.630
absolutely a worse practice to in any way to try and skew

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:06.190
the results of a survey. And in fact, the case about retail,

00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:10.059
you will be fired if you even so much as tell a customer

00:43:10.079 --> 00:43:14.259
that they might get a survey. You can't even mention it.

00:43:14.279 --> 00:43:20.199
So you know, we try and show that we have this concept of

00:43:20.219 --> 00:43:22.429
all companies say they really care about the customer

00:43:22.429 --> 00:43:26.429
experience. Maybe 10% really care about it. You know,

00:43:26.449 --> 00:43:30.539
another 40% are kind of semi - care about it and then a lot

00:43:30.559 --> 00:43:32.239
of them don't really care. It's like motherhood and apple

00:43:32.239 --> 00:43:33.239
pie.

00:43:33.239 --> 00:43:37.690
So, we have this concept of believers, seekers, and then non

00:43:37.710 --> 00:43:40.809
- believers. And I guess our feeling is we try and share the

00:43:40.829 --> 00:43:43.569
best practices of the believers - the ones who have shown we

00:43:43.569 --> 00:43:48.889
really understand it and some will gravitate. And then, you

00:43:48.909 --> 00:43:51.599
know, we really work closely with the seekers to help them

00:43:51.599 --> 00:43:54.519
and then at the end of the day we feel better.

00:43:54.539 --> 00:43:59.119
If somebody gets, you know, 10% better and they don't become

00:43:59.139 --> 00:44:02.089
the absolute best, that's still 10% better. So, I mean we

00:44:02.089 --> 00:44:05.079
sort of help people wherever they are on that journey. And

00:44:05.099 --> 00:44:11.579
you know, we don't lower our prices. So, we end up heavily

00:44:11.599 --> 00:44:13.940
skewed toward the believers because we have the best

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:16.569
product. But the functionality that we have is most

00:44:16.589 --> 00:44:19.039
interesting to people who really care about this vs. people

00:44:19.039 --> 00:44:20.119
who are paying lip service to it.

00:44:20.119 --> 00:44:21.119
Right.

00:44:21.119 --> 00:44:25.210
One interesting thing about our product and the topic of

00:44:25.230 --> 00:44:29.150
culture is - we were talking about this before this started.

00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:33.369
Stories are an incredible part of the culture. The stories

00:44:33.389 --> 00:44:36.239
about the founders like you were mentioning one.

00:44:36.259 --> 00:44:41.199
But one thing we've found about our customers is there's

00:44:41.219 --> 00:44:43.829
always a question that's an open - ended question and you

00:44:43.829 --> 00:44:46.859
get these really precious customer comments from time to

00:44:46.879 --> 00:44:51.259
time and some of them become, you know, just legend in the

00:44:51.279 --> 00:44:52.279
organization.

00:44:52.279 --> 00:44:57.900
So, you stop getting just the co - founder's stories or you

00:44:57.920 --> 00:45:01.000
know, the superstar employee stories and you start getting

00:45:01.019 --> 00:45:04.059
the customer stories. "The customer had this experience."

00:45:04.079 --> 00:45:06.179
You hear things for example, about Nordstrom and what

00:45:06.179 --> 00:45:08.889
Nordstrom's employees will do for their customers.

00:45:08.909 --> 00:45:12.079
And so, we found that our platform ironically does

00:45:12.099 --> 00:45:15.869
strengthen culture as those things become almost urban

00:45:15.889 --> 00:45:17.799
legends within an organization.

00:45:17.819 --> 00:45:22.130
That's great. Okay. So, last question and then we'll open up

00:45:22.150 --> 00:45:23.389
for

00:45:23.409 --> 00:45:27.139
[inaudible]. So, Jawbone is known for cutting edge - and

00:45:27.159 --> 00:45:29.659
this is a quote - "Cutting edge design coming up with

00:45:29.659 --> 00:45:32.099
products that people don't know they need, but then they

00:45:32.099 --> 00:45:36.329
can't live without." I'm like, "Wow! That's great." I was

00:45:36.349 --> 00:45:38.219
like, "I want one of those."

00:45:38.239 --> 00:45:40.769
So, this sounds a lot like the culture of innovation,

00:45:40.789 --> 00:45:46.690
engineering, you know, design being ahead of customers. But

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:49.489
it seems like there are a lot of stories of the danger with

00:45:49.489 --> 00:45:52.239
that kind of culture is that you can become ancillary and

00:45:52.239 --> 00:45:55.389
insensitive to customers. And it's kind of like, you know, "

00:45:55.409 --> 00:45:58.210
Well, we know best." You know, "You'll know what you want

00:45:58.210 --> 00:46:03.059
when we tell you what it is." And then you sort of lose your

00:46:03.079 --> 00:46:04.480
market.

00:46:04.500 --> 00:46:08.900
How do you either balance that, protect against that

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:12.359
possibility and think about, you know, "We want to listen,

00:46:12.379 --> 00:46:15.289
but we also want to push

00:46:15.309 --> 00:46:16.309
[inaudible]

00:46:16.309 --> 00:46:18.519
That's a great question. I think that's an existential

00:46:18.519 --> 00:46:22.480
challenge for a company like us, right? Because so much of

00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:25.250
what we do - especially as we push into new categories and

00:46:25.250 --> 00:46:27.500
new markets - you're looking around a corner and you're

00:46:27.500 --> 00:46:29.000
trying to guess what people want.

00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:31.750
So, we do. It's on multiple different vectors. It's also in

00:46:31.750 --> 00:46:34.500
terms of how do we manage our investment against those new

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:37.989
things and sort of take it step by step, and phase. Test

00:46:38.009 --> 00:46:41.509
something, see if it works. Go behind. It's a little bit of

00:46:41.529 --> 00:46:44.279
what we call a chasing model. So, if it's working really

00:46:44.279 --> 00:46:47.529
well we double down and push into it. So, those are kind of

00:46:47.529 --> 00:46:49.279
just financial risk things that we do.

00:46:49.279 --> 00:46:52.279
But you know, fundamentally I think that what we try to do -

00:46:52.279 --> 00:46:55.279
and this is sort of the way we build products actually - is

00:46:55.279 --> 00:46:59.210
we make a map and we don't do MRD's, or PRD's, or any of

00:46:59.210 --> 00:47:01.429
those things. We don't do focus groups. We don't do any of

00:47:01.429 --> 00:47:02.429
it.

00:47:02.429 --> 00:47:05.179
We basically sit around - and there's a core group of about

00:47:05.179 --> 00:47:08.429
six people in the company who work on this - and we make a

00:47:08.429 --> 00:47:11.429
map of, "So, here's the way that the world works today with

00:47:11.429 --> 00:47:14.179
all the kinds of devices that we interact in." We're the

00:47:14.179 --> 00:47:16.929
friction points in the way that we use these devices or

00:47:16.929 --> 00:47:19.929
other people. Now you can get a lot of data from customers

00:47:19.929 --> 00:47:22.429
and many people around. What are the friction points? Where

00:47:22.429 --> 00:47:25.429
are the things that don't work? You know, would they have to

00:47:25.429 --> 00:47:27.929
push a button here, take that thing out or whatever?

00:47:27.929 --> 00:47:30.929
And then we start to say, "Okay. Well, if we could solve

00:47:30.929 --> 00:47:33.929
that problem and innovate, you know, 'Do we need to push the

00:47:33.929 --> 00:47:36.429
Silicon guys or do we need to build networking technology

00:47:36.429 --> 00:47:39.179
and support?'" And then we start to push on all those

00:47:39.179 --> 00:47:41.929
different threads. And then we build stuff say, "How are we

00:47:41.929 --> 00:47:43.429
resolving against this experience continuum?" And

00:47:43.429 --> 00:47:45.679
ultimately, it's about making and using technology to make

00:47:45.679 --> 00:47:48.179
things easier for people or allow them to communicate in

00:47:48.179 --> 00:47:50.179
places they didn't before, and things like that.

00:47:50.179 --> 00:47:52.679
And so, that's one of the issues from the product

00:47:52.679 --> 00:47:55.179
perspective we try to resolve when we say, "It's about

00:47:55.179 --> 00:47:57.179
making things easier and resolving experiences. Wouldn't it

00:47:57.179 --> 00:47:59.429
be great if you could do this right here?"

00:47:59.429 --> 00:48:01.679
So, it is a little bit Star Treky with

00:48:01.679 --> 00:48:04.179
[inaudible] and some of that stuff. But I think you're going

00:48:04.179 --> 00:48:06.929
to make some mistakes for sure and it's how you structure

00:48:06.929 --> 00:48:07.929
yourself to reiterate quickly...

00:48:07.929 --> 00:48:09.929
Right. And be able to recover from that.

00:48:09.929 --> 00:48:12.929
Yeah, and be able to recover. And you know, that's one of

00:48:12.929 --> 00:48:15.929
the reasons we almost died a few times and it just changes.

00:48:15.929 --> 00:48:21.170
So, we have this sort of multi - headed system intact.

00:48:21.190 --> 00:48:22.690
Can I add something on that?

00:48:21.940 --> 00:48:22.940
Yeah.

00:48:22.940 --> 00:48:25.690
Cause our customer sees it two ways. There are two big

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:28.440
components. One is service and one is product. And on the

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:30.940
service side, a lot of our companies, they have strong

00:48:30.940 --> 00:48:33.440
cultures so they delegate a lot of responsibility to their

00:48:33.440 --> 00:48:36.359
front lines and they don't micromanage. Some franchise

00:48:36.379 --> 00:48:39.079
organizations you'll go into and you'll feel like you're

00:48:39.079 --> 00:48:41.329
getting the scripted, "Did you find everything you need?"

00:48:41.329 --> 00:48:44.859
But others you actually have a real conversation. So, we

00:48:44.879 --> 00:48:47.880
tend to serve those groups. And what happens is different

00:48:47.900 --> 00:48:53.710
people try different things, you know, at different stores,

00:48:53.730 --> 00:48:56.569
or different banks, or whatever it is. And then our system

00:48:56.589 --> 00:48:58.089
you start to see customer satisfaction...

00:48:57.589 --> 00:48:58.589
Right.

00:48:58.589 --> 00:49:01.199
is spiking, customer satisfaction is dropping. What's going

00:49:01.219 --> 00:49:04.400
on here? It turns out this manager came up with a really

00:49:04.420 --> 00:49:09.609
innovative solution to a problem. I've got a great example.

00:49:09.629 --> 00:49:13.179
I'm going to try and camouflage who it is, but basically

00:49:13.199 --> 00:49:16.679
customer satisfaction across this company tanked. Then one

00:49:16.699 --> 00:49:20.099
guy looked at it and he realized that "salesperson" and "

00:49:20.119 --> 00:49:22.940
rude" kept coming up with the low scores for a particular

00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:26.059
product. So, he asked the salespeople. And they said, "Yeah,

00:49:26.079 --> 00:49:28.639
it's off warranty and everybody's really pissed off when

00:49:28.639 --> 00:49:30.619
they find out it's off warranty."

00:49:30.639 --> 00:49:34.069
So, he told them when they all came to work this next day -

00:49:34.089 --> 00:49:36.690
they all owned this particular product themselves. He took

00:49:36.710 --> 00:49:41.000
it from them for a day and he gave it back 24 hours later

00:49:41.019 --> 00:49:44.920
and customer satisfaction in that store spiked because now

00:49:44.940 --> 00:49:48.909
people came in with this product off warranty and all of the

00:49:48.929 --> 00:49:52.150
sales reps that they ran into said, "That sucks! That

00:49:52.170 --> 00:49:56.440
happened to me the other day. I am so sorry! Bad news is,

00:49:56.460 --> 00:49:59.369
you know, it's off warranty. The good news is I can..."

00:49:59.369 --> 00:50:00.449
And it was the same message...

00:50:00.449 --> 00:50:01.449
Right.

00:50:01.449 --> 00:50:05.469
delivered with genuine empathy and that was it. And it was

00:50:05.489 --> 00:50:07.389
like a brilliant, smart

00:50:07.409 --> 00:50:10.259
[inaudible]. And then somebody at corporate saw this spike,

00:50:10.279 --> 00:50:13.529
went in and found out what it was, told the rest of the

00:50:13.529 --> 00:50:15.389
organization, scores went up everywhere.

00:50:15.409 --> 00:50:17.329
So you know, that's on the service side. On the product

00:50:17.329 --> 00:50:18.329
side...

00:50:18.329 --> 00:50:19.359
It's a lot of listening.

00:50:19.359 --> 00:50:20.359
Yeah.

00:50:20.359 --> 00:50:21.609
You've got to listen carefully.

00:50:21.609 --> 00:50:23.609
But it's also allowing people who are in the front lines...

00:50:23.609 --> 00:50:24.609
Right.

00:50:24.609 --> 00:50:27.480
communicating with the marketplace to use their judgment to

00:50:27.500 --> 00:50:30.349
deal with the nuance. And on the product side, Hyatt is one

00:50:30.349 --> 00:50:34.589
of our customers and they started a new concept, "Hyatt

00:50:34.609 --> 00:50:37.239
Place" for the thirty - something business travelers. And

00:50:37.259 --> 00:50:40.569
they did a whole bunch of focus groups to get rid of things

00:50:40.589 --> 00:50:43.670
like the front desk, and all these things that are sort of

00:50:43.690 --> 00:50:46.279
standard in hotels. And then they put it out there with us

00:50:46.279 --> 00:50:49.829
and they were just monitoring. They had a couple of hotels

00:50:49.849 --> 00:50:52.279
and they were trying, you know, "Should we serve this kind

00:50:52.279 --> 00:50:55.529
of food or that kind of food?" in terms of the product side

00:50:55.529 --> 00:50:57.779
and just really, really tracking it and being very

00:50:57.779 --> 00:51:00.029
innovative, and quickly changing if it wasn't working.. And

00:51:00.029 --> 00:51:04.839
so just being very, very nimble and not expecting to get it

00:51:04.859 --> 00:51:34.859
100% right when they rolled it out.

00:56:01.539 --> 00:56:31.539
So you know, I think it comes down to the ways to get that

01:01:09.679 --> 01:01:16.609
listening and feedback.

01:01:16.629 --> 01:01:17.629
Right.

01:01:17.629 --> 01:01:47.629
[Informal talk] I'm watching time. I probably should have

01:13:25.920 --> 01:13:55.920
kind of punctuated this point and come to the sort of

01:14:37.649 --> 01:14:42.279
closing part a little earlier. But is there anything - any

01:14:42.300 --> 01:14:46.409
insights about culture or startups - that, you know, remain

01:14:46.429 --> 01:14:50.019
unsaid that you would just be not satisfied if you didn't

01:14:50.039 --> 01:14:54.529
leave this group with that last pearl?

01:14:54.550 --> 01:14:56.359
So, we've got about five minutes.

01:14:56.380 --> 01:14:57.380
Yeah.

01:14:57.380 --> 01:14:58.380
Anything

01:14:57.630 --> 01:14:58.630
[Overlapping]

01:14:58.630 --> 01:15:02.659
maybe potentially a little controversial, but what I have

01:15:02.679 --> 01:15:07.189
learned is the value of the team vs. the value of

01:15:07.210 --> 01:15:10.449
individuals. And at the end of the day if you're like me,

01:15:10.449 --> 01:15:12.949
and you've gone to great schools, and you've gone to

01:15:12.949 --> 01:15:14.949
business school, and you've worked in certain organizations

01:15:14.949 --> 01:15:17.449
- unless you've played a team sport you know nothing about

01:15:17.449 --> 01:15:19.559
teamwork really. You probably think you're doing teamwork

01:15:19.559 --> 01:15:22.429
when you all huddle together, divide up tasks, run off, do

01:15:22.449 --> 01:15:25.319
your tasks, come back together. That's not teamwork.

01:15:25.340 --> 01:15:30.769
So, I think that we hire a lot of people just like me in our

01:15:30.789 --> 01:15:33.539
company. And so, we're all learning to be better true team

01:15:33.539 --> 01:15:36.289
players and I think a book that was really influential for

01:15:36.289 --> 01:15:42.299
me is called "Tribal Leadership". It's by a professor out of

01:15:42.319 --> 01:15:45.069
USC and he looked at all these different organizations, and

01:15:45.090 --> 01:15:48.209
listened to the language that they had, and he divided

01:15:48.229 --> 01:15:51.799
cultures into five different cultures. I won't bore you with

01:15:51.819 --> 01:15:55.399
it, but it really talks at the end of the day about when you

01:15:55.420 --> 01:15:58.659
have true teamwork you get to a stage where you're not

01:15:58.679 --> 01:16:01.829
really going to have these interdepartmental conflicts cause

01:16:01.849 --> 01:16:04.909
everybody is going to be working toward that same goal.

01:16:04.929 --> 01:16:09.459
And he finds that in only about 20% of companies. And the

01:16:09.479 --> 01:16:13.679
vast majority are all in a level where it's all about, "I'm

01:16:13.699 --> 01:16:18.679
great, others not so much." Sorry about that. You know, the

01:16:18.699 --> 01:16:21.339
ingoing assumption is, "Don't' trust until they earn your

01:16:21.359 --> 01:16:24.549
trust" rather than "Trust until they don't earn your trust."

01:16:24.569 --> 01:16:27.329
The ingoing assumption is to withhold information rather

01:16:27.349 --> 01:16:31.379
than to share information. It's a great way read and if you

01:16:31.399 --> 01:16:34.079
read it will probably resonate with you in different places

01:16:34.099 --> 01:16:35.099
that you've worked.

01:16:35.099 --> 01:16:38.049
And so, that really made me think about how little I

01:16:38.069 --> 01:16:40.999
understood about true teamwork and how many of the people

01:16:41.019 --> 01:16:44.229
around me didn't really understand it either. So..

01:16:44.250 --> 01:16:49.549
I guess the way I would leave it is, you know, when we talk

01:16:49.569 --> 01:16:52.069
about good culture it's a very enormous concept. What's good

01:16:52.069 --> 01:16:54.819
for me may be different for Andrew, may be different based

01:16:54.819 --> 01:16:57.899
on different organizations, different requirements and so

01:16:57.920 --> 01:17:00.789
forth. But what I always find interesting - particularly

01:17:00.809 --> 01:17:03.079
when you're interviewing people - is people can't always

01:17:03.079 --> 01:17:05.579
describe what the perfect culture is, but they can very

01:17:05.599 --> 01:17:08.709
clearly tell you what bad cultures they've been part of.

01:17:08.729 --> 01:17:11.599
So you know, what are the behaviors? It could be the 201st

01:17:11.599 --> 01:17:15.399
experience they had with an executive that was completely

01:17:15.420 --> 01:17:18.969
out of character with, you know, what a company says. But

01:17:18.989 --> 01:17:23.659
you know, it's a matter of feeling what's right for you at

01:17:23.679 --> 01:17:26.139
that moment in time, what's right for the employees who are

01:17:26.139 --> 01:17:29.209
there. But so much of good culture is just common sense.

01:17:29.229 --> 01:17:32.669
There are just certain things that you don't do. You treat

01:17:32.689 --> 01:17:36.169
people respectfully. You know, you have to empower them. You

01:17:36.189 --> 01:17:39.189
have to allow them to make decisions. As long as there's a

01:17:39.189 --> 01:17:42.609
core baseline of values that you subscribe to, everything

01:17:42.630 --> 01:17:46.169
else you put on top is okay. Just the bad stuff is

01:17:46.189 --> 01:17:50.929
universal.

01:17:50.949 --> 01:17:56.389
Okay. So, I think a lot has already been said. I would say

01:17:56.409 --> 01:18:00.879
that for some of you that are considering entrepreneurship

01:18:00.899 --> 01:18:03.969
especially a lot of you that have worked for whether it's

01:18:03.989 --> 01:18:06.919
private equity banking, consulting you haven't really had to

01:18:06.939 --> 01:18:09.279
think of this developing of the culture.

01:18:09.300 --> 01:18:12.719
One thing to think about as a founder and entrepreneur is

01:18:12.739 --> 01:18:15.839
your visibility. It's extraordinarily powerful and more so

01:18:15.859 --> 01:18:19.269
than you can even imagine. There is, you know, a magnifying

01:18:19.289 --> 01:18:23.369
glass on everything you do, everything you say.

01:18:23.389 --> 01:18:24.389
Good and bad.

01:18:24.389 --> 01:18:27.749
Good and bad. And a lot of people talked about feedback.

01:18:27.769 --> 01:18:29.889
Feedback's extraordinarily important. You've got to be

01:18:29.889 --> 01:18:33.179
careful about doing it in public. Anything that you say -

01:18:33.199 --> 01:18:37.349
some small, constructive feedback - is magnified 10 times by

01:18:37.369 --> 01:18:40.149
the person that's receiving it just because of your role as

01:18:40.149 --> 01:18:43.149
the founder in the company. That's a hard thing to adjust to

01:18:43.149 --> 01:18:45.649
when you're out of business school that, "Wow! These people

01:18:45.649 --> 01:18:49.149
are hanging on my every word." If I come in the office and I

01:18:49.149 --> 01:18:51.899
look stressed out, that stresses other people out. If I come

01:18:51.899 --> 01:18:54.649
in the office and I'm happy that makes other people happy.

01:18:54.649 --> 01:18:57.569
It's a very surreal experience when you start to realize how

01:18:57.569 --> 01:19:00.399
visible you are and how much people are looking at your

01:19:00.420 --> 01:19:04.129
every action, and every action that you take is creating

01:19:04.149 --> 01:19:05.149
that culture.

01:19:05.149 --> 01:19:06.149
Last word.

01:19:06.149 --> 01:19:11.509
I totally agree with that. And I mean, it's a constant

01:19:11.529 --> 01:19:14.699
evolution in learning because that visibility and that sort

01:19:14.719 --> 01:19:18.249
of how people hang on what you say is changing all the time.

01:19:18.269 --> 01:19:20.559
It's different and different people react to things

01:19:20.579 --> 01:19:22.949
differently. I would totally agree with that.

01:19:22.969 --> 01:19:26.309
The thing I would say to take away that probably people won'

01:19:26.329 --> 01:19:31.649
t tell you about culture is that it's very, very dynamic,

01:19:31.670 --> 01:19:36.899
right? And there are core values that Scott talks about that

01:19:36.920 --> 01:19:40.289
are just fundamental things that you've got to decide what

01:19:40.309 --> 01:19:42.809
you want to do and those things will be part of the

01:19:42.809 --> 01:19:43.809
character forever.

01:19:43.809 --> 01:19:46.309
But in terms of the systems, and incentives, and rewards,

01:19:46.309 --> 01:19:50.809
all that stuff changes a lot. And you know, I think you've

01:19:50.829 --> 01:19:53.329
got to be prepared for that reiteration cycle especially in

01:19:53.329 --> 01:19:57.309
the entrepreneurial world. It's okay. It's okay and I would

01:19:57.329 --> 01:20:00.550
say to always remember to take a little bit of a longer term

01:20:00.550 --> 01:20:04.559
view than feels necessarily comfortable.

01:20:04.579 --> 01:20:08.399
Okay. On that note, please join me in thanking our panel.

01:20:08.420 --> 01:20:09.420
And...

